<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Piast dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 960–<a href="/years/1675">1675</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Piasts</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Piast dynasty was the first historical ruling house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a>, baptised in 966, to the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iii-the-great">Casimir III the Great</a> in 1370. After 1138 the realm fragmented into duchies held by rival Piast branches in Silesia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuyavia">Kuyavia</a> and elsewhere. The Masovian line died out in 1526 and the last Silesian Piast, George William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legnica">Legnica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brzeg">Brzeg</a>, in 1675.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty.jpg" alt="Piast dynasty" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wereszczyński · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:COA_Piast_dynasty.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The early dukes and kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> are said to have regarded themselves as descendants of the Piast the Wheelwright, a figure known from tradition, first mentioned in the Deeds of the Princes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, written c. 1113 by Gallus Anonymus. The term Piast dynasty is not documented in contemporary sources; the Polish historian Adam Naruszewicz introduced it in a historical work.</p><p>The first Piasts, probably of Polan descent, appeared around 940 at the stronghold of Giecz in Greater Poland. Shortly afterwards they moved their residence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gniezno">Gniezno</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a> ruled the Civitas Schinesghe from about 960. At various times the dynasty also ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatias</a>, as well as part of Ruthenia and the Hungarian Spiš region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Polish monarchy had to deal with the expansionist policies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. Piast rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a>, Casimir I the Restorer and Władysław I Herman tried to protect the state by treaties, oaths of allegiance and marriage alliances with the Ottonian and Salian dynasties. Other powerful neighbours were the Bohemian Přemyslid dynasty, the Árpáds and their Anjou successors in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, the State of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>.</p><p>The dynasty was decisively weakened by the fragmentation that followed the Testament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Bolesław III Wrymouth</a> in 1138. For nearly 150 years the Polish state was divided into several duchies, and Piast dukes fought for the throne at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-iii-the-old">Mieszko III the Old</a>, Władysław III Spindleshanks and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leszek-the-white">Leszek I the White</a> were each crowned, only to be overthrown. The senior Silesian branch went its own way and from the 14th century was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lands-of-the-bohemian-crown">Bohemian Crown</a>.</p><p>After the Polish royal line died out in 1370, the crown passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary">Louis I of Hungary</a>, son of Casimir the Great's sister Elizabeth Piast. The Masovian branch became extinct with the death of Duke Janusz III in 1526, and the last ruling duke of the Silesian Piasts, George William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legnica">Legnica</a>, died in 1675.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>About 1295 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/przemysl-ii">Przemysł II</a> used a coat of arms with a white eagle, later called the Piast Eagle. In the 14th century the Silesian Piasts used an eagle modified by a crescent, which became the coat of arms of the Duchy of Silesia. The Piasts intermarried with several noble lines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and held numerous titles, some within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, and Piast daughters became queens consort in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and elsewhere. The Jagiellonian kings who ruled after the death of Casimir IV were also descended in the female line from a daughter of Casimir the Great.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Piast dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under George II the castle was rebuilt in Renaissance style, with a gatehouse adorned with statues of the <b>Piasts</b>, and a gymnasium was founded in 1569.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-brzeg">Duchy of Brzeg</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cieszyn <b>Piasts</b> ruled for over three centuries.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-cieszyn">Duchy of Cieszyn</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Piast</b> lines died out and disputes followed, Głogów was granted to the Jagiellonian Sigismund, who ruled it from 1499 until 1506 before becoming King of Poland.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-glogow">Duchy of Głogów</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the testament of Boleslaw III in 1138, his son Mieszko III the Old received Greater Poland, with Poznan and Gniezno, where the <b>Piast</b> state had begun.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-greater-poland">Duchy of Greater Poland</a></li><li><blockquote>They were then reunited under Bolko II the Small, who resisted Bohemian overlordship, ruling as an independent <b>Piast</b> prince while other Silesian dukes became Bohemian vassals.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-jawor">Duchy of Jawor</a></li><li><blockquote>Koźle belonged to the Upper Silesian lands of the Duchy of Bytom, ruled by a branch of the <b>Silesian Piasts</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-kozle">Duchy of Koźle</a></li></ul></section>
